  "OverallSummary": "This case involves Dalmore Group LLC, a broker-dealer primarily engaged in acting as broker-dealer of record for private offerings. From January 2019 to December 2022, Dalmore violated Regulation Best Interest's Care Obligation by failing to establish and maintain a supervisory system reasonably designed to ensure compliance with its suitability and best interest obligations in the sale of private placements to retail customers. The firm failed to establish proper procedures for investigating offerings, identifying who was responsible for conducting due diligence, or maintaining records of investigations. In many cases, Dalmore either had no record of due diligence, had incomplete checklists, or relied solely on issuer-provided information without independent verification. The firm's WSPs did not reference Reg BI until February 2021, eight months after it took effect, and even then only provided general information without tailoring guidance to Dalmore's business. The case also details multiple other violations, including failing to prevent misuse of material non-public information, failing to fingerprint 45 non-registered associated persons, failing to report or timely report outside business activities, violating FINRA's communication standards through misleading statements on websites and in videos, and willfully violating Exchange Act Rule 10b-9 by failing to specify a date for meeting a contingency offering minimum and improperly releasing approximately $3 million in investor funds before the $5 million minimum was met. The firm was censured, fined $375,000, and required to certify remediation of these issues within 90 days."
